### When should you use a loop over the Stream API?

Streams and loops are complementary tools, not replacements. Use each where it naturally fits.

### Case 1: Index-based access

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class IndexAccessDemo {
    record DailyBalance(String date, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<DailyBalance> balances = List.of(
            new DailyBalance("2024-01-01", new BigDecimal("100000")),
            new DailyBalance("2024-01-02", new BigDecimal("105000")),
            new DailyBalance("2024-01-03", new BigDecimal("98000"))
        );

        // LOOP: compare adjacent elements naturally
        for (int i = 1; i < balances.size(); i++) {
            BigDecimal today = balances.get(i).balance();
            BigDecimal yesterday = balances.get(i - 1).balance();
            if (today.compareTo(yesterday) < 0) {
                System.out.println(balances.get(i).date() + ": balance dropped");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
2024-01-03: balance dropped
```

### Case 2: Checked exceptions per element

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class CheckedExceptionDemo {
    record Transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {}
    static class TransferException extends Exception {
        TransferException(String msg) { super(msg); }
    }

    static void execute(Transfer t) throws TransferException {
        if (t.amount().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0) {
            throw new TransferException("Amount exceeds limit: " + t.amount());
        }
        System.out.println("Transferred Rs." + t.amount());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transfer> transfers = List.of(
            new Transfer("SAV2001", "SAV2002", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Transfer("SAV2001", "CUR3001", new BigDecimal("200000")),
            new Transfer("CUR3001", "SAV2003", new BigDecimal("30000"))
        );

        // LOOP: clean try/catch per element
        for (Transfer t : transfers) {
            try {
                execute(t);
            } catch (TransferException e) {
                System.out.println("Failed: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Transferred Rs.50000
Failed: Amount exceeds limit: 200000
Transferred Rs.30000
```

### Case 3: Complex break with mutable accumulator

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class BudgetBreakDemo {
    record Order(String id, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Order> orders = List.of(
            new Order("ORD-001", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Order("ORD-002", new BigDecimal("80000")),
            new Order("ORD-003", new BigDecimal("120000")),
            new Order("ORD-004", new BigDecimal("30000"))
        );

        BigDecimal budget = new BigDecimal("200000");
        BigDecimal spent = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        List<Order> executed = new ArrayList<>();

        for (Order order : orders) {
            if (spent.add(order.amount()).compareTo(budget) > 0) {
                break; // stop: budget would be exceeded
            }
            spent = spent.add(order.amount());
            executed.add(order);
        }

        System.out.println("Executed: " + executed.size() + ", spent: Rs." + spent);
    }
}
```

```text
Executed: 2, spent: Rs.130000
```

### When should you use streams instead?

Streams are better for declarative pipelines, grouping, aggregation, and parallelisation.

```java
// Grouping: impossible to beat with a for-loop
Map<String, BigDecimal> totalByBranch = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Account::branch,
        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Account::balance, BigDecimal::add)));
```

### What are the decision rules?

| Need | Use |
|------|-----|
| Index access (adjacent comparison) | for-loop |
| In-place mutation (JPA dirty checking) | for-loop |
| Checked exception per element | for-loop |
| Break with mutable accumulator | for-loop |
| Nested loops with labeled break | for-loop |
| filter-map-collect-reduce | Stream |
| groupingBy, partitioningBy | Stream |
| parallelStream | Stream |
| Read-only analytics | Stream |

### What should a senior engineer know?

Forcing streams where loops are natural leads to worse code: convoluted workarounds for checked exceptions, side-effect-heavy forEach, and AtomicReference hacks. Conversely, forcing loops where streams shine leads to verbose boilerplate. In interviews, showing that you know when NOT to use streams demonstrates deeper understanding.
